# Direct dependencies for the Polson demo web app. Hand-edited; this is the file you change.
#
# The pinned, hash-locked file that actually gets installed is requirements.txt, which is GENERATED
# from this one. Do not edit requirements.txt by hand. See README.md for the commands.
#
# Every line here is a name being trusted, and each one drags in a transitive tree. Keeping the list
# short is a security property, not tidiness: it is the difference between a dependency graph a
# person can review and one they can only hope about. Prefer doing without over adding a package.
#
# Names are easy to get subtly wrong, and a wrong name is a live typosquat risk. Each is written
# below with its project page so it can be checked against the real repository before first install.

# Web framework and ASGI server.
#   fastapi   https://github.com/fastapi/fastapi
#   uvicorn   https://github.com/encode/uvicorn
#
# Plain uvicorn, not uvicorn[standard]: the extra pulls in websockets, httptools, watchfiles and
# more, none of which a Server-Sent Events app needs. Fewer packages, less surface.
fastapi
uvicorn

# Server-side HTML templating. Autoescaping is what stops model-authored text from becoming markup
# in the page, so it must stay on and `|safe` must stay away from agent or judge input.
#   jinja2    https://github.com/pallets/jinja
jinja2

# Server-Sent Events response type for Starlette/FastAPI. SSE is one-way server to client, which is
# all the thinking/artifact stream needs, and it reconnects on its own.
#   sse-starlette   https://github.com/sysid/sse-starlette
sse-starlette

# Syntax highlighting for the JS artifacts, rendered server-side. Pygments escapes as it highlights,
# so the code panel cannot become an injection vector.
#   pygments  https://github.com/pygments/pygments
pygments

# The agent SDK. Drives the Gemini agent that does the design work and attaches our MCP server.
#   google-antigravity   https://github.com/google/antigravity-sdk-python
#
# The distribution name is confirmed, not guessed. `reference/projects/antigravity-sdk-python-0.1.15`
# declares `name = "google-antigravity"` in its pyproject.toml, and the package looks *itself* up by
# that exact string at runtime — `importlib.metadata.version("google-antigravity")` in
# connections/local/local_connection.py. A name a package uses to find itself cannot be the wrong
# name for it.
#
# It pulls a real tree: absl-py, google-genai>=1.0, mcp>=1.0,<3.0, pydantic>=2.0, uvicorn>=0.46,
# websockets>=12.0. Read that part of the requirements.txt diff especially — `websockets` arrives
# here despite being deliberately excluded above by choosing plain `uvicorn` over `uvicorn[standard]`,
# which is exactly the kind of thing the diff is for.
google-antigravity

# NOT named here, but present anyway:
#
#   python-multipart  — this list does not ask for it, and it arrives regardless: `mcp` depends on
#                       it, and `mcp` is not optional once the agent SDK is in. So the intent behind
#                       leaving it out — no multipart parser, because that is a parser for untrusted
#                       bytes — is not achieved, and pretending otherwise would be worse than saying
#                       so. What still holds is that *we* do not post multipart: HTMX sends
#                       urlencoded forms, which Starlette parses without it. Do not add an upload
#                       without deciding that separately.
#
# Reviewing the lock diff is what surfaced this, which is the point of the step. Anything appearing
# under a `# via` you did not expect deserves the same treatment.
